1. Start MySQL
~/mysql/server/bin/mysqld \
  --basedir=$HOME/mysql/server \
  --datadir=$HOME/mysql/data \
  --socket=$HOME/mysql/mysql.sock \
  --port=3306 \
  --bind-address=127.0.0.1 \
  --console

Keep this terminal open.

2. Connect to MySQL from another WSL terminal
~/mysql/server/bin/mysql \
  --socket=$HOME/mysql/mysql.sock \
  -u root -p

Enter your MySQL root password.

3. Connect phpMyAdmin to this MySQL

If phpMyAdmin is already installed, its configuration should point to:

Host:     127.0.0.1
Port:     3306
User:     root
Password: your MySQL password

Then open phpMyAdmin in your browser, typically:

http://localhost/phpmyadmin
If phpMyAdmin is running on Windows

If your phpMyAdmin is Windows/XAMPP phpMyAdmin while MySQL is running inside WSL, 127.0.0.1:3306 may not work depending on your WSL networking setup.

First test from WSL:

ss -tlnp | grep 3306

You should see:

127.0.0.1:3306

Then test MySQL TCP connection:

~/mysql/server/bin/mysql -h 127.0.0.1 -P 3306 -u root -p

If that works, your MySQL server is accepting TCP connections.

Recommended first step: start MySQL with the command in Step 1, then run the TCP test in Step 2. If that works, I'll give you the exact config.inc.php settings for phpMyAdmin.
====================================================================================
If you want MySQL to run in the background
#Bash
~/mysql/server/bin/mysqld \
  --basedir=$HOME/mysql/server \
  --datadir=$HOME/mysql/data \
  --socket=$HOME/mysql/mysql.sock \
  --port=3306 \
  --bind-address=127.0.0.1 \
  > ~/mysql/mysql.log 2>&1 &

Check that it's running:
ps aux | grep '[m]ysqld'
And:
ss -tln | grep 3306
Then you can use the same MySQL connection command:

~/mysql/server/bin/mysql -h 127.0.0.1 -P 3306 -u root -p

For now, keep your current terminal open and use a second terminal. Your MySQL server is already running correctly.
